# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# REMARK[chinese] = (phonetics, reason)
REMARK = {
"頭曼": (
 "頭 <i>do</i> → <i>du</i>: the initial is direct; o and u are not separable at the ~50% vowel "
 "recovery §7 reports. 曼 <i>mɑn</i> → <i>man</i>: exact, and the coda -n is written, because -n is "
 "one of the six codas the Chinese syllable can carry. One vowel change in two syllables. The rival "
 "reading <i>tümen</i> needs ü, which the Later Han script cannot write at all.",
 "<i>duman</i> / <i>tuman</i> / <i>touman</i> “smoke, fog, mist” is one word across Kāşgarî, "
 "the Codex Cumanicus and modern Turkish. The standard identification is <i>tümen</i> “ten "
 "thousand”, which is absent from the Cuman vocabulary entirely."),

"稽粥": (
 "稽 <i>kʰei</i> → <i>ki</i>: an aspirated velar is the ordinary vehicle for a source k, and the "
 "diphthong <i>ei</i> for <i>i</i> is routine. 粥 <i>tśuk</i> → <i>çük</i>: tś- is the default "
 "character class for a source affricate (37% in the Sanskrit corpus), and the coda -k is actually "
 "written, the strongest part of the reading, since a stop coda goes unwritten 30% of the time. "
 "Only ü is unavailable and surfaces as u.",
 "<i>kiçig / kiçik / kiçük</i> “small, younger” is attested three times independently: "
 "Kāşgarî has <i>kiçik</i> and <i>kiçük</i> with the derivative <i>kiçiglik</i>, the "
 "<i>Kutadgu Bilig</i> has <i>kiçig oğlan erken</i> “when he was a small boy” (1823), and "
 "the Codex has <i>Kečak</i> glossed <i>paucus</i>. 稽粥 is the personal name of Laoshang chanyu, "
 "Modu's son and successor, so “the younger” fits the succession the Chinese sources record."),

"軍臣": (
 "軍 <i>kun</i> → <i>kün</i>: initial and coda direct; ü is unwritable and surfaces as u. "
 "臣 <i>dźin</i> → <i>çin</i>: dź is the voiced partner of tś, and voicing is leaky in both "
 "directions in every corpus measured, so a source ç is reachable through it; the -n coda is written.",
 "<i>kün</i> “sun, day”. The Seljuk regnal names Kaykhusraw and Kayqubad carry an "
 "association of royal solar radiance through their <i>kay-</i> element."),

"詹師廬": (
 "詹 <i>tśam</i> → <i>çam</i>: tś- is the default affricate spelling and the -m coda is written. "
 "師 <i>ṣi</i> → <i>şı</i>: the retroflex sibilant is the standard vehicle for ş. 廬 <i>liɑ</i> → "
 "<i>lı</i>: note that Chinese l- also carries source r-: in the paper's correspondence counts an r- was written l- in 12 of 14 attestations, so this syllable is genuinely ambiguous between -lı and -rı.",
 "The same solar association proposed for <i>Künçin</i>, from a stem of shining or dazzling."),

"呴犁湖": (
 '呴 <i>huo</i> → <i>ka</i>, 犁 <i>lei</i> → <i>lı</i>, 湖 <i>gɑ</i> → <i>k</i>: three onsets, each taken from the majority correspondence, with nothing inserted and no coda discarded. The velar class covers h-, k- and g- alike, so the first and third syllables cost nothing.',
 'Kāşgarî glosses <i>kalık</i> “hava, gök, sema” (air, sky, heaven). The word belongs to the same semantic field as 撑犁, the one Xiongnu term the Chinese sources gloss (天 “heaven”), so a ruler bearing it would not be isolated in the record. A second exact fit, <i>kılık</i> “disposition, manner”, is available if the sky reading is thought too convenient.'),
"伊稚斜": (
 "伊 <i>ʔi</i> → <i>i</i>: a glottal onset is the standard way to write a vowel-initial syllable. "
 "稚 <i>ḍi</i> → <i>di</i>: retroflex ḍ for d is routine. 斜 <i>ja</i> → <i>ya</i>: this character "
 "class emits <i>ya</i> more strongly than any other single-syllable correspondence in the record. "
 "All three steps are default.",
 "<i>idi</i> “lord, master”, attested in Old Turkic. Compare Murad I's epithet "
 "<i>Hüdavendigâr</i>, “the sovereign”, a title of the same shape and force."),

"烏維": (
 "烏 <i>ʔɑ</i> → <i>a</i>: vowel-initial, direct. 維 <i>wi</i> → <i>bı</i> is the weak step: the "
 "Chinese character carries a w-, and while Turkic b- and w- alternate, the transcription reads more "
 "naturally as <i>avı</i> than <i>abı</i>. This is one of only five items in the record decoded "
 "entirely from character-specific evidence, so the mismatch cannot be attributed to a gap in the data.",
 "<i>av</i> “hunt”, <i>avcı</i> “hunter”. <i>Avcı</i> names an occupation; "
 "<i>abı</i> is proposed as naming a trait. <i>Avcı</i> is also the epithet of Mehmed IV."),

"且鞮侯": (
 "且 <i>tsɑ</i> → <i>ça</i>: ts- for a source affricate is a minority spelling but a real one, 15% "
 "in the Sanskrit corpus and 9% in Ligeti. 鞮 <i>te</i> → <i>tı</i>: direct. 侯 <i>go</i> → "
 "<i>gka</i> asks a single Chinese syllable to carry a consonant cluster it structurally cannot, so "
 "the reading adds material the transcription does not have.",
 "<i>çat-</i> “to strike together, to clash”. Compare Selçuk Bey, whose name has been read "
 "as “perpetually ready for the fight”."),

"狐鹿姑": (
 "姑 <i>kɑ</i> → <i>ga</i> is well supported by the velar correspondence. The other two syllables "
 "are not: 狐 <i>ɣuɑ</i> → <i>kur</i> supplies an -r that Later Han has no coda slot for, and "
 "鹿 <i>lok</i> → <i>lu</i> discards a coda that was written. Adding an unwritable segment and "
 "dropping a written one in the same word pulls in opposite directions.",
 "狐鹿姑 captured the Han general Li Guangli and held the confederation together through a hard "
 "decade, so an epithet of binding fits the record. Compare the Göktürk ruler Kapgan Kagan, "
 "<i>kapgan</i> “the encloser”."),

"壺衍鞮": (
 "壺 <i>gɑ</i> → <i>ka</i>: given a Later Han voiced g-, the source sound is more often k than g, so "
 "the devoicing is normal rather than a liberty. 衍 <i>jan</i> → <i>yan</i>: direct, coda written. "
 "鞮 <i>te</i> → <i>tı</i>: direct. All three steps are default or better, phonetically one of the "
 "cleaner readings here.",
 "<i>kay-</i> “to slide, to slip away”. Parallel in sense to Bayezid I's <i>Yıldırım</i>: "
 "a natural force taken as a regnal epithet."),

"虛閭權渠": (
 '虛 <i>kʰɨɑ</i> → <i>ka</i>, 閭 <i>liɑ</i> → <i>l</i>, 權 <i>gyan</i> → <i>kan</i>: the first three characters match exactly, including the written -n coda, at zero minority steps. Only 渠 <i>gɨɑ</i> is left unaccounted for, and it may carry a separate title element.',
 '<i>kalkan</i> “shield” is in Kāşgarî and in the Codex Cumanicus as <i>Kalkani</i>, “scutum ejus”. A weapon or armour name is an ordinary steppe formation, and it needs no compound to be read.'),
"握衍朐鞮": (
 "握 <i>ʔɔk</i> → <i>ok</i> is exact, with the -k coda written, and 衍 <i>jan</i> → <i>yan</i> and "
 "鞮 <i>te</i> → <i>tı</i> are direct. The weak point is 朐 <i>guo</i> → <i>kutuğ</i>, which expands "
 "one Chinese syllable into two Turkic ones; nothing in the corpora licenses that expansion.",
 "<i>ok</i> “arrow” doubles as a division of the confederation in later Turkic usage (the "
 "<i>On Ok</i>, “ten arrows”, of the Western Turks) which is what makes a territorial "
 "reading available. <i>kut</i> is “heavenly favour, fortune”."),

"呼韓邪": (
 "呼 <i>hɑ</i> → <i>ka</i>: an h/k substitution, which the Mongolian corpus supports strongly (a "
 "source k or q was written with an h- character 27% of the time). 韓 <i>gɑn</i> → <i>gan</i>: exact, "
 "coda written. 邪 <i>ja</i> → <i>ya</i>: the strongest single correspondence in the record. The "
 "written sequence <i>ha gan ya</i> becomes <i>ka-gan-ya</i> on one change.",
 "呼韓邪 commanded the left flank before becoming ruler, so the Chinese record itself supplies the "
 "office that the reading names."),

"郅支": (
 "郅 <i>tśit</i> → <i>çit</i>: tś- is the default affricate vehicle and the -t coda is written. "
 "支 <i>tśe</i> → <i>çi</i>: default. Both syllables use the majority correspondence and neither "
 "requires an inserted or deleted segment.",
 "<i>çit</i> / <i>çiz-</i>, of marking or carving a boundary. 郅支 broke away westward and set up a "
 "rival seat, so a name of division fits the career, though it is equally possible that the career "
 "suggested the name."),

"復株累若鞮": (
 '復株累 <i>buk ṭio lui</i> → <i>büktel</i>: the b-, the -k coda, the dental and the liquid all fall in place across the three characters, at zero minority steps, with only the front rounded vowel unwritable. One attested word covers the whole prefix rather than an invented two-element compound. 若鞮 → <i>Inakt</i> is treated in §S1.4.',
 'Kāşgarî gives <i>büktel</i> as “orta boylu; yassı arkalı, oturamaklı”: of middling height and flat-backed. A name from a bodily description is a well-attested Turkic type; compare the Hungarian royal name <i>Şişman</i>.'),
"搜諧若鞮": (
 '搜 <i>so</i> → <i>sar</i>: a sibilant onset, which the correspondence counts make obligatory: a source k, q or g was written with a Chinese sibilant in 0 of 2,124 attestations, while Chinese sibilant characters transcribe s, š, č or ś in 98% of 837 cases. The final -r is not written and could not be: Later Han carries no -r in any coda position. 諧 <i>gɛi</i> → <i>ıg</i>: a velar onset, direct. Nothing is inserted, and no character is read as a sound it cannot carry. 若鞮 → <i>Inakt</i> is treated in §S1.4.',
 '<i>sarıg</i> “sarı, sarı renk” (yellow) is in Kāşgarî, and survives as modern Turkish <i>Sarı</i>, still current both as a name and as an epithet. Color epithets are a documented Turkic naming type: the same dictionary has <i>çakır</i> “grey-eyed”, and <i>Çal</i> “dappled, grey” is proposed for 烏珠留若鞮 on the same footing. Ninety-one attested headwords fit this two-syllable frame, so it does not determine the reading; <i>sarıg</i> is offered as the most plausible of them, with nothing in the transcription standing against it.'),
"車牙若鞮": (
 "車 <i>tśʰa</i> → <i>çöge</i>: the aspirated affricate is a good vehicle for ç, but ö is unwritable "
 "and a second syllable is added. 牙 <i>ŋa</i> → <i>ok</i>: a velar-nasal initial standing for a "
 "vowel-initial syllable is unsupported.",
 "<i>çöge</i> of height or eminence, with the same <i>ok</i> element."),

"烏珠留若鞮": (
 '烏 <i>ʔɑ</i> is vowel-initial, so the reading begins at 珠 <i>tśo</i> → <i>ç</i>, with 留 <i>liu</i> → <i>l</i>: an affricate written with the default tś- character and a liquid, both majority correspondences. Nothing is inserted, and no character is read as a consonant it does not carry.',
 '<i>çal</i> is “alaca, kır” in Kāşgarî: dappled, grey. Color epithets are a persistent Turkic naming pattern, as <i>çakır</i> “grey-eyed” shows in the same dictionary.'),
"烏累若鞮": (
 '烏 <i>ʔɑ</i> → <i>u</i>: a glottal-onset character is the standard way to write a vowel-initial syllable. 累 <i>lui</i> → <i>lu</i>: a liquid, direct. Two characters, two segments, nothing supplied that the transcription does not carry.',
 'The Codex Cumanicus glosses <i>Oulu</i> as <i>magnus</i>. <i>Uluğ</i> “great” is among the most common elements in Turkic titulature, which is both the strength of this reading and its weakness: a very common word is an easy target to hit.'),
"呼都而尸道皋若鞮": (
 "Eight characters, and correspondingly the least constrained reading in the file: the longer the "
 "string, the more ways there are to split it into Turkic elements, and the split itself is doing "
 "most of the work rather than any individual correspondence. 呼 <i>hɑ</i> → <i>öz</i> and "
 "皋 <i>kou</i> → <i>uluğ</i> are the two largest jumps.",
 "<i>öz</i> “self, core” and <i>uluğ</i> “great”, both common Old Turkic elements "
 "in titulature."),

"攣鞮": (
 "攣 <i>lyan</i> → <i>el</i> moves the liquid from the onset of the Chinese syllable to the coda of "
 "the Turkic one; no corpus shows that kind of metathesis. 鞮 <i>te</i> → <i>indi</i> expands one "
 "syllable into two. The reading is driven by the sense rather than by the transcription.",
 "<i>el</i> “realm, state” with the definite past in <i>-di</i>: literally “he founded "
 "the realm”. This is the clan name as the <i>Shiji</i> and <i>Hanshu</i> write it."),

"虛連題": (
 "虛 <i>kʰɨɑ</i> → <i>kıya</i> is reachable. 連 <i>lianᴮ</i> → <i>lığ</i> adds an unwritten coda, and "
 "題 <i>de</i> → <i>el-indi</i> carries a whole two-syllable word on one character. Three characters "
 "are being asked to yield five syllables.",
 "The same clan name as 攣鞮, in the form the <i>Hou Hanshu</i> gives, with the <i>kaya</i> element "
 "recurring from 虛閭權渠."),

"單于": (
 "單 <i>dźan</i> → <i>kan</i> is the weak step: a voiced affricate standing for a velar stop is not a "
 "correspondence any of the three corpora show. 于 <i>wɑ</i> → <i>apa</i> expands one syllable into "
 "two. This is one of the five items decoded entirely from character-specific evidence, which makes "
 "the mismatch harder to attribute to sparse data.",
 "<i>apa</i> “elder, ancestor” is attested in Old Turkic titulature. This is the central "
 "title of the whole record and the most contested: readings from three language families have been "
 "proposed for it."),

"撑犁": (
 "撑 <i>ḍaŋ</i> → <i>tang</i>: retroflex ḍ for t is routine and the -ŋ coda is written. "
 "犁 <i>lei</i> → <i>lı</i>: direct. Both steps are default. The same two syllables equally support "
 "<i>tengri</i>, which the Chinese gloss 天 and the lexicon search of §6.3 both favour: Kāşgarî "
 "returns it 4th, the Codex 2nd.",
 "<i>tang</i> of dawn or light, read as “bearing divine light; sacred”. Offered as an "
 "alternative to the standard identification with <i>tengri</i>, not as an improvement on it."),

"當戶": (
 '當 <i>tɑŋ</i> → <i>ton</i>: direct, with the -ŋ coda written. 戶 <i>gɑᴮ</i> → <i>ga</i>: a velar onset, direct. Both characters do work, and the pair is the standard device for writing a medial nasal-plus-velar: in the Sanskrit corpus every source word with a medial <i>ŋg</i> or <i>ŋk</i> is written exactly this way, a -ŋ coda followed by a velar onset: 13 cases of 13, as in 央具梨 for <i>aṅguli</i>, 商羯羅 for <i>śaṅkara</i>, 鴦耆羅 for <i>aṅgiras</i>. Phonetically this is among the least demanding readings in the file: nothing inserted, no coda discarded, no character left over. The vowels are free as always, so <i>Tonga</i>, <i>Tunga</i> and <i>Tanga</i> are equally available.',
 '<i>tonga</i> “Siberian tiger, leopard” is the word in the name of the epic hero Alp Er Tonga. Big-cat epithets are a persistent Turkic pattern: Alp Arslan (<i>arslan</i>, lion), Baybars (<i>bars</i>, a large cat). Note that a search of the two lexicons on this frame does not return <i>tonga</i> itself; what it returns is <i>tanuk</i> “witness”, <i>tañuk</i> “a gift presented to a khan” and <i>tünek</i> “prison”, of which the second belongs to court vocabulary.'),
"閼氏": (
 "閼 <i>ʔɑt</i> → <i>e</i>: a vowel-initial character, but with a -t coda that is written and that "
 "the reading discards. 氏 <i>dźeᴮ</i> → <i>ce</i>: direct. Another of the five fully "
 "character-anchored items, so the discarded -t is a real cost rather than an artefact.",
 "<i>ece</i> “queen”, which survives in modern Turkish with roughly this sense. 閼氏 is the "
 "title of the chanyu's principal consort."),

"羯": (
 "A single character. 羯 <i>kɨat</i> → <i>kıyat</i>: the k- is default and the -t coda is written, so "
 "the consonant frame k…t is secure and only the medial glide is free. Fully character-anchored.",
 "<i>kıyat</i>, of flooding. The Jie are recorded as a group within or beside the Xiongnu; the "
 "reading is offered for the ethnonym only, and beside rather than against the standing one: Pulleyblank and Vovin read the ethnonym as <i>*ket</i> and connect it with Proto-Yeniseian <i>*keʔt</i> “person, human being” (Ket <i>keʔt</i>, Yug <i>keʔt</i>, Kott <i>hit</i>, Arin <i>kit/qit</i>, Pumpokol <i>kit</i>), which is the Yeniseian self-designation (CAJ 44/1, p. 91)."),

"匈奴": (
 "匈 <i>huoŋ</i> → <i>hung</i>: direct, with the -ŋ coda written. 奴 <i>nɑ</i> is discarded rather "
 "than read, on the assumption that 奴 “slave” is a Chinese pejorative appended to the name "
 "rather than part of it.",
 "Dropping 奴 is a common move in the literature and is not original here; what is proposed is only "
 "the vocalisation of the remaining syllable."),

"胡祿": (
 '胡 <i>gɔ</i> → <i>kü</i>: the devoicing is normal, a Later Han voiced g- standing for a source k more often than for a source g; ü cannot be written and surfaces as o. 祿 <i>lok</i> → <i>lüg</i>: the -k coda is written. The consonant frame k–l–k is secure and only the vowels are free.',
 '<i>külüg</i> “famous, renowned” is an Old Turkic epithet of praise, recorded by Clauson; note that the form is <i>külüg</i>, not <i>külük</i>, and it is not a headword in either lexicon used here. <b>Two objections stand.</b> First, the Chinese-side record gives the meaning of 胡祿 as “quiver”, not a term of praise, though that gloss is itself marked low-confidence and chronologically shaky. Second, the reading the gloss wants is barred by the transcription: the one Turkic quiver word whose skeleton fits is <i>okluk</i> (Kāşgarî, glossed <i>sadak</i>), and it is vowel-initial, whereas 胡 carries a voiced velar. In the Sanskrit corpus a source vowel-initial syllable was written with a zero or glottal onset in 96% of 183 cases, and with a velar in none. So this is an item where the Chinese gloss names a target, a Turkic word exists for it, and the spelling rules that word out, recorded because a negative of that shape is worth more than a resemblance.'),
"骨都侯": (
 '骨 <i>kuət</i> → <i>kut</i>: default velar onset with the -t coda written, the tightest single syllable in this file. 都 <i>tɑ</i> then begins with the same dental, and Table 8 of the paper measures what that means: at a junction where a written coda is followed by the same-place onset, the source word carries that consonant once rather than twice in 121 of 140 cases (86%). So 骨都 transcribes <i>kuta</i>, not <i>kutta</i>. 侯 <i>go</i> → <i>-k</i> closes it, a Later Han voiced g- standing for a source k more often than for a source g.',
 "<i>kut</i> is “kut, uğur, devlet, baht, talih, saadet” in Kāşgarî (heavenly favour, fortune, sovereign grace) and Clauson records the verb <i>kutad-</i> “to be blessed”, the stem behind the <i>Kutadgu Bilig</i>. <i>Kutak</i> itself is <b>not attested</b>: it would be a deverbal or diminutive formation in -(A)k on that stem, which is a live suffix but not one witnessed on this word. Compare <i>Qutalmış</i>, ancestor of the Seljuks of Rum. Note also that <i>kut</i> is the Turkic candidate for 孤塗, where the Chinese gloss 子 “son” fits the Yeniseian proposal better and not this one."),
"且渠": (
 "且 <i>tsɑᴮ</i> → <i>ça</i>: ts- for a source affricate is a minority spelling but an attested one, "
 "15% in the Sanskrit corpus and 9% in Ligeti, against a tś- character as the default at 37%. "
 "渠 <i>gɨɑ</i> → <i>ka</i>: a Later Han voiced g- stands for a foreign k more often than for a "
 "foreign g. Both steps are permitted by the measured correspondences.",
 "<i>çak-</i> “to strike fire from flint, to sting, to slander”, which Clauson describes as "
 "onomatopoeic in character and of violent action, physical or mental. No <i>çaka</i> headword exists "
 "in Kāşgarî, the Codex or Clauson, only the verb and its derivatives <i>çakmak</i>, <i>çakır</i>, "
 "<i>çakılmak</i>, so <i>Çaka</i> would have to be the -A converb of that stem, and a bare converb "
 "serving as an office title is not a demonstrable pattern. The 11th-century emir Çaka Bey of Smyrna "
 "(Byzantine Τζαχᾶς, <i>fl.</i> 1081–93) shows that <i>çak-</i> could yield a personal name, but "
 "that is a personal name set against a title. The Chinese sources do not record what the office of "
 "且渠 did, so no sense can be tested against a gloss."),

"徑路": (
 "徑 <i>keŋᶜ</i> → <i>kıng</i>: default velar onset with the -ŋ coda written. 路 <i>lɑᶜ</i> → <i>ra</i>: a Chinese l- for a source r-, the direction those counts show in 12 of 14 attestations. The Turkic word's final -k is not written, which Table 7 of the paper licenses at 30%.",
 "<b>Not our reading.</b> Friedrich Hirth compared 徑路 with Turkic <i>kıŋrak</i> in <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (1908), pp. 65–70, calling it the oldest Turkish word on record. Kāşgarî glosses <i>kıñrak</i> as a knife like a butcher's cleaver, for cutting meat and dough, and the Chinese sources describe 徑路 as a blade used in oath-taking, so gloss and referent agree across unconnected sources. <b>Turkic origin, on Clauson's analysis.</b> Clauson derives <i>kıŋra:k</i> as a deverbal noun from an unattested <i>*kıŋra:-</i>, denominal from <i>kıñır</i>, in the sense of something curved or something that cuts crookedly, so the word is motivated inside Turkic by the standard authority rather than by us. Kāşgarî's index carries the same family (<i>kıñru</i> “askew”, <i>kañrak</i> “palate”, <i>koñur-</i> “to wrench, bend back”), and the Codex glosses <i>Kingir</i> as <i>curvus</i>. Clauson also records the word alive in Teleut as <i>kıŋırak</i> and Kirghiz <i>kıŋarak / kıŋırak</i>, a rough two-edged knife for cutting felt and scraping hides. <b>Two cautions.</b> Kāşgarî's text at III 382 reads <i>kırpa:k</i>, corrected in the margin to <i>kıŋra:k</i>, so the form rests on an emendation; and of the family it is <i>kıñır</i> that fits the transcription exactly, while <i>kıñrak</i>, the member meaning a knife, needs the unwritten final -k allowed at 30%. <b>Iranian origin.</b> The object has also been derived from an Iranian word for a short sword, the <i>akinakes</i> type, historically plausible, since the Xiongnu's western neighbors were Iranian-speaking. But 路 is a liquid-onset character, so the channel requires a liquid in second position, and the akinakes frame (k–n–k) has none. As stated, that account fails on the frame. Two cautions there too: we have not seen the exact Iranian etymon its proponents advance, and the channel shows what a transcription permits, never what it requires. §9.1 of the paper sets both origins out."),
"服匿": (
 "服 <i>buk</i> → <i>buk</i>: exact, including the written -k coda. 匿 <i>ṇɨk</i> → <i>nik</i>: "
 "retroflex ṇ for n is routine and the second -k is also written. This is the closest fit to its own "
 "transcription of anything in this file: no segment inserted, none discarded.",
 "A clay jar (Turkish <i>küp</i>). One of the few Xiongnu material terms the Chinese sources "
 "preserve, which is what makes it worth a reading at all."),

"甌脫": (
 "甌 <i>ʔo</i> → <i>o</i>: vowel-initial, direct. 脫 <i>tʰuɑt</i> → <i>tuyat</i>: the aspirated t- is "
 "direct and the -t coda is written; the only addition is the medial -y-.",
 "The border watchtowers, or the empty zone between Xiongnu and Han territory, for which the Chinese "
 "sources use 甌脫."),

"冒頓": (
 "The frame is <b>b/m – k/g – t/d – n</b>, and <b>both codas are written</b>, which makes it one of the tightest in the record. 冒 <i>mək</i> → <i>bögü</i>: three things happen, and each is licensed. The m- stands for a source b-, which in the Later Han corpus happens in 3 of 22 cases, 13.6%. The written -k stands for the source g. And the final vowel of <i>bögü</i> is not written, one character covering two source syllables, a device that occurs in 47 of the 1,017 verified pairs, always with a stop-coda character doing the compressing, as 竭 covers <i>-gata</i> in <i>tathāgata</i> and 弗 covers <i>-putra</i> in <i>śāriputra</i>. <b>There is also a character-level precedent, not merely a rate:</b> 冒 and 默 have the identical Later Han reading <i>mək</i>, and 默啜 is read <i>Bögü-Çor</i>, the personal name of Qapaghan Qaghan. That syllable is on record writing <i>bögü</i> in a Turkic ruler's name. 頓 <i>tuənᶜ</i> → <i>tın</i>: a dental onset with the -n coda written, nothing inserted, nothing discarded.",
 "<b>Bögü Tın, “sage spirit”.</b> Kāşgarî glosses <i>bügü</i> as “bilgin, akıllı, hakim”; Clauson says the word “seems to connote both wisdom and mysterious spiritual power”, and it was borrowed early into Mongolian as <i>böge</i>, the ordinary word for a shaman. It appears in regnal styles across the record: <i>Teŋri Bögü Teŋriken</i>, Bögü Qaghan of the Uyghurs, Bögü-Çor of the Türks. The second element <i>tın</i> is “ruh, nefes” in Kāşgarî and <i>anima</i> in the Codex. Both halves are attested independently of any Chinese source, and the shape, two nouns in apposition, is the commonest Turkic name pattern. <b>Against the standard reading.</b> <i>Baγatur</i> “hero” has been the field's answer for a century. It fails on the point the corpus decides cleanly: it ends in -r against a written -n, a spelling attested in 1 of 37 opportunities, where the scribe's normal choices were an open character or a -t. <b>What is not settled.</b> The compound <i>bögü tın</i> is not itself attested, only its two elements; the vowel of the second is unrecoverable, so <i>tün</i> “night”, <i>tan</i> “dawn” and <i>tun</i> “rest” are equally available and the preference for <i>tın</i> is semantic rather than phonological; and the tone category of 頓 is unused. §9.2 of the paper argues this reading in full."),
"若鞮": (
 '若 <i>ńɑk</i> → <i>ınak</i>: a palatal nasal onset with the -k coda written. 鞮 <i>te</i> → <i>-t</i>: the final stop is not written, and Table 7 of the paper licenses that at 30%. One objection stands and the paper states it: if the source was <i>ınakt</i>, the opening <i>ı-</i> is not written and 若鞮 has no room for it.',
 '<b>One of the two readings argued in the paper itself, at §9,</b> and it is listed here so this file can be read as a single list with the standard visible in it. <i>Inak</i> is glossed <i>fidelis</i> “faithful” in the Codex Cumanicus; the <i>Hou Hanshu</i> glosses 若鞮 as 孝 “filial”; the plural in <i>-t</i> is an Old Turkic formation used with titles (<i>tegin</i> → <i>tegit</i>); and Clauson records Chagatay <i>inak</i> as “a royal representative or senior minister”, so a court title on this root is what the word ordinarily does.'),

"撑犁孤塗單于": (
 'Six characters forming the full ceremonial title, and the only item in the record the Chinese sources gloss in full. It decomposes into three units that each have their own row here: 撑犁 <i>ḍaŋ lei</i>, 孤塗 <i>kuɑ dɑ</i> and 單于 <i>dźan wɑ</i>. No separate reading is proposed for the compound; whatever the three parts are read as, the whole follows.',
 'The <i>Hanshu</i> glosses the phrase as “Son of Heaven, the Chanyu”, with 撑犁 = 天 “heaven” and 孤塗 = 子 “son”. That makes it the single most valuable item in the record, because both the form and the meaning are recorded, and it is the evidence on which most of the language-attribution argument has rested for a century.'),

"孤塗": (
 'The frame is <b>k/g/h – t/d</b> with the vowels free, and both codas unwritten, so a source -r, -l, -s or -z could hide in either. 66 attested headwords fit it.',
 '<b>The Chinese sources gloss this one: 子 “son”.</b> That is a rare gift and a hard test. The 66 candidates include <i>kut</i> “heavenly favour, fortune”, <i>kat</i> “layer”, <i>kata</i> “time, occasion”, <i>ketü</i>, <i>Kerti</i> <i>verus</i>, <i>kötü</i>, and not one of them means “son”. The Turkic proposal in the literature is <i>qut</i> “fortune”, which fits the sound and not the gloss. The Yeniseian side is weaker than it is usually reported to be: Pulleyblank compared the word with Arin <i>bïkjàl</i> “son”, and Vovin, arguing for a Yeniseian Xiongnu, holds that Arin form to be isolated in the family, the other languages reflecting Proto-Yeniseian <i>*puʔ-b</i> “son” (Ket <i>hiʔp</i>, Yug <i>fiʔp</i>, Kott <i>fup</i>, Assan <i>pup</i>), and finds no Altaic etymology either (CAJ 44/1, p. 91). So no reading is proposed here: the item on which the Chinese gloss bears most directly is the one both camps leave open.'),

"屠耆": (
 '屠 <i>dɑ</i> → <i>do</i>: a dental onset, direct. 耆 <i>gɨ</i> → <i>ğru</i>: a velar onset, with the -r unwritten, which is forced rather than permitted, since Later Han carries no -r in any coda position. 144 attested headwords fit the frame, so the transcription does little to narrow the choice; the vowels are free as always.',
 '<b>Not our reading.</b> The Turkic-hypothesis literature reads 屠耆 as <i>*toγrï</i>, and the Codex Cumanicus has <i>Togru</i> glossed <i>rectus</i>. The Chinese sources gloss 屠耆 as 賢 “worthy, wise”, and it appears in 左右屠耆王, the Wise Kings of the Left and Right. <b>The senses meet more closely than “straight” suggests.</b> Clauson derives <i>toğuru: / toğru:</i> as a gerund of <i>toğur-</i>, physically “straight” and then metaphorically “straight, honest, upright, true”, and that moral sense is old rather than modern: a fourteenth-century glossary renders it with Arabic <i>al-tiqa</i> “trustworthy, honest”, and <i>toğru ayt-</i> with <i>ṣadaqa</i> “to tell the truth”. The word survives across the whole Turkic family and as modern Turkish <i>doğru</i>, “straight, correct”. <b>The family also yields names.</b> Kāşgarî records that men are called <i>Toğrıl</i> after a bird of prey; an <i>Alp Toğrul Tégin</i> appears in a Uyghur list of proper names; and the first Seljuk sultan bore the name. §9.1 of the paper tests this case alongside 徑路.'),
"谷蠡": (
 'The frame is <b>k/g/h – k/g – l/r</b>, with the -k of 谷 written and the coda of 蠡 open. It is unusually tight: only <b>8</b> attested headwords fit, against 66 for 孤塗 and 144 for 屠耆. That frame, however, assumes one of three readings. The Later Han table gives 谷 as <i>kok</i>, <i>jok</i> and <i>lok</i>, and the reading printed above is the first row. The office is conventionally called the Luli Kings, which points at <i>lok</i>, and on that reading the frame becomes <b>l – l/r</b> and the eight candidates below do not apply. The candidate set for this item needs recomputing from a settled reading before any name is proposed for it.',
 'On the <i>kok</i> reading the eight are <i>kagıl</i> “a fresh willow branch used to tie vines”, Codex <i>Kagal</i> <i>lentus</i> “slow, pliant”, Codex <i>Kukel</i> <i>gramen</i> “grass”, <i>Hakil</i> <i>prudentia</i> (an Arabic loan, so excluded on the same grounds as <i>kadı</i>), <i>kafgar</i>, <i>kekre</i>, and two proper names. None is obviously a title. 谷蠡 names a senior office, the Luli Kings ranking below the Wise Kings, so the tightness of the frame makes this a good target: a reading that fits has few competitors.'),
}
